<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="activity-origami_box">
  <title>Modeling and Optimizing an Origami <em>Masu</em> Box</title>
<introduction>
  <p>
    The <em>masu</em> is both a traditional unit of measurement (approximately
    180<nbsp/>mL) used in Japan for <em>measuring</em> sake or rice, and a
    square-based wooden box traditionally used for <em>serving</em> rice or
    sake.
  </p>

  <p>
    There is also an origami construction called a <em>masu</em> box. We will
    experiment to see if we can fold a <em>masu</em> box so that it would hold
    as much rice as possible.
  </p>

  <p>
    First we will fold a <q>standard</q> <em>masu</em> box together, then, in
    groups, you will fold several <em>masu</em> boxes whose heights (and
    therefore widths) are different from that of the standard <em>masu</em> box
    and analyze the results.
  </p>
</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="sec-procedure">
  <title>Procedure</title>

  <p>
          <xref ref="fig-guide_step_one"/> through <xref ref="fig-guide_step_ten"/> is a step-by-step pictorial guide to
    help you fold the boxes. The crucial steps for making boxes of different
    heights are <alert>Step<nbsp/>3</alert> and <alert>Step<nbsp/>5</alert>. If
    you fold short of the center the box will be shorter (and wider). If you
    fold past the center the box will be taller (and narrower).
  </p>
      <sidebyside widths="13% 13% 13% 13% 13%" margins="auto" >
          <figure xml:id="fig-guide_step_one">
<caption>Step 1: Crease and return twice</caption><image decorative="yes"  source="Origami_Masu_Box_Step_01"/>
</figure>
          <figure xml:id="fig-guide_step_two">
<caption>Step 2: Fold four corners to center</caption><image decorative="yes"  source="Origami_Masu_Box_Step_02"/>
</figure>
          <figure xml:id="fig-guide_step_three">
            <caption><alert>Step 3: Fold two sides to height and return</alert></caption><image decorative="yes"  source="Origami_Masu_Box_Step_03"/>
</figure>
          <figure xml:id="fig-guide_step_four">
<caption>Step 4: Open two corners</caption><image decorative="yes"  source="Origami_Masu_Box_Step_04"/>
</figure>
          <figure xml:id="fig-guide_step_five">
            <caption><alert>Step 5: Fold other sides to height</alert></caption><image decorative="yes"  source="Origami_Masu_Box_Step_05"/>
</figure>
      </sidebyside>
      <sidebyside widths="13% 13% 13% 13% 13%" margins="auto" >
          <figure xml:id="fig-guide_step_six">
<caption>Step 6: Lift both sides and one end</caption><image decorative="yes"  source="Origami_Masu_Box_Step_06"/>
</figure>
          <figure xml:id="fig-guide_step_seven">
<caption>Step 7: Fold flap to center</caption><image decorative="yes"  source="Origami_Masu_Box_Step_07"/>
</figure>
          <figure xml:id="fig-guide_step_eight">
<caption>Step 8: Raise other end</caption><image decorative="yes"  source="Origami_Masu_Box_Step_08"/>
</figure>
          <figure xml:id="fig-guide_step_nine">
<caption>Step 9: Fold flap to center</caption><image decorative="yes"  source="Origami_Masu_Box_Step_09"/>
</figure>
          <figure xml:id="fig-guide_step_ten">
<caption>Step 10: Completed box</caption><image decorative="yes"  source="Origami_Masu_Box_Step_10"/>
</figure>
      </sidebyside>

  <p>
    <alert>Note: The size of the folds in Step<nbsp/>3 and Step<nbsp/>5 must be the same!</alert>
  </p>

  <p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Each group should <alert>fold at least three boxes</alert> of
          differing heights.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Some suggestions for boxes to fold are:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                a box of height 1<nbsp/>inch,
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                a box of height 1.5<nbsp/>inches,
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                a box of height 2.5<nbsp/>inches, and
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                a box of height 2.75<nbsp/>inches.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    For each of the boxes you fold:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Plan how to fold the box in order to get the desired height. (For
          example, you may decide to mark your paper with a pencil to help you
          choose the positions of your folds.)
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          After folding, measure the box carefully to determine its dimensions
          and calculate its volume. (We will record the data from these
          measurements as a class.)
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Carefully unfold the box to reveal the fold pattern. (It should look
          similar to Figure<nbsp/><xref ref="fig-fold_pattern_short,fig-fold_pattern_standard,fig-fold_pattern_tall," text="local"/>.)
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>


      <sidebyside widths="30% 30% 30%" margins="auto">
          <figure xml:id="fig-fold_pattern_short">
<caption>Short</caption><image decorative="yes" source="masuShort"/>
</figure>
          <figure xml:id="fig-fold_pattern_standard">
<caption>Standard</caption><image decorative="yes" source="masuStandard"/>
</figure>
          <figure xml:id="fig-fold_pattern_tall">
<caption>Tall</caption><image decorative="yes" source="masuTall"/>
</figure>
      </sidebyside>
</subsection>

<subsection>
  <title>Analysis</title>

  <p>
    After examining and measuring the fold patterns for your group's boxes:
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Make a prediction about the dimensions of an origami <em>masu</em> box
          of maximum volume. (Do you think it will be taller than it is wide,
          wider than it is tall, or will its height and width be the same? If
          taller or wider, by how much?)
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Use the fold pattern to find a relationship between the width and the
          height (your relationship may involve the length of the diagonal,
          <m>d=12\sqrt{2}\approx 16.97056\text{ in.}</m> )
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Determine your <em>decision variable</em>.
          <alert>Stop here to discuss.</alert>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Turn your relationship into a function, <m>f</m>, returning the other
          dimension as a function of the decision variable. That is, your
          function, <m>f</m>, should satisfy one of <m>w = f(h)</m> or
          <m>h = f(w)</m>.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Use your function, <m>f</m>, as a starting point to develop a
          <em>volume</em> function, <m>g</m>, so that the volume, <m>V</m>, is
          given as a function of the decision variable.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Determine what values of the decision variable are reasonable inputs
          for your functions ( <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> ) in this situation.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Use calculus to find the value of the height that will make the volume
          as large as possible.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Is the <q>standard</q> box optimal? If not, is the optimal box taller
          or shorter?
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          How does your result compare to your prediction? Explain what might
          account for any discrepancies, or, if your prediction was accurate,
          explain what insight led to your prediction.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          If you wish (and you have enough paper) fold a box of the optimal
          dimensions.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>
</subsection>
</section>
